Chapter 1: The Last Voice of Boston Rock



Endings have a way of turning even the most resolute among us into storytellers full of empty vows. We vow to bow out with grace, to utter some timeless wisdom, to make our final moments ring out like a perfect chord. But here I sit—Maxanne Sartori, age seventy-three, in the same dusky Studio C where I first fell under Aerosmith's spell in the spring of 1973—and all I can summon are memories of two kids whose voices never found their way onto my playlist.  
WBCN goes dark at midnight. Fifty-six years of blasting through the Boston airwaves as the city's rock heartbeat, and tomorrow morning our frequency will carry nothing but automated Christian contemporary. Around me rests the equipment I refused to abandon when the world went digital: turntables with porcelain platters dulled by decades of use, cart machines whose amber plastic holds the DNA of every Boston legend, mixing boards scarred by a thousand impassioned tweaks. Soon these relics will be sold for scrap or consigned to glass display cases in museums, where teenagers will gape at them like fossilized relics, oblivious to the fact that these machines once made democracy possible, that radio could be a lifeline, that voices could matter more than the market research charts they were squeezed into.  
"Maxanne?" The program director's voice crackles through the thin metal of the intercom. He's twenty-eight, sneakers gleaming with logo-stamped logos, and he's convinced rock history began the day Kurt Cobain picked up a guitar. "Four hours left. Make them count."  
Make them count. As if four hours of vinyl and tape could encapsulate all that WBCN meant to this city—to the underground shows puked up by sweaty basements, to the bands who learned to play in drafty garages, to every kid who found a reflection of themselves in our static. As if I could unspool fifty-six years of back-room politics, fistfights at the station door, midnight epiphanies over headphones into one neat little package bound with nostalgia's ribbon.  
I squeeze the bridge of my nose, the faint tang of vinyl dust tickling my nostrils, and adjust my headphones—the same battered Sony cans I've worn since 1971, straps fraying and mic held together with a single strip of electrical tape and more stubbornness than sense. My fingers glide over the spines of the records I've chosen for tonight's farewell: each album jacket edged with fingerprints and a fine film of studio dust. I place them on the spindle with the quiet reverence of a mourner laying flowers at a grave. The ON AIR sign blooms red above me, a final heartbeat in fluorescent neon.  
"Good evening, Boston," I murmur into the mic that has been my confessional and my pulpit since Nixon's shadow still loomed over this country. "This is Maxanne Sartori, calling one last time."  
Outside Studio C, the Prudential Tower's rotating beacon cuts through the black October sky, a solitary sentinel in a city transformed by glass and steel. Where once dive bars overflowed with hopeful guitarists and sweat-stained dreamers, now polished cocktail lounges echo with ambient chill. The Combat Zone's neon haze—once dangerous, defiant, alive—has given way to high-rise condos that hum with the quiet drone of yuppie comfort. Boston still glitters, but it's a different kind of spark now.  
I reach for a small glass of bourbon, its amber liquid quivering with promise. Radio regulations technically forbid on-air drinking, but tonight the rules feel like suggestions tossed into wind. One sip warms me like an old friend, loosening the knots of nostalgia. I'll need more courage for the stories to come.  
"I've been your guide through Boston rock since long before some of you knew what 'rock' even meant," I continue, settling into the familiar cadence that has steered this city through broken amplifiers and broken hearts. "Back in '73, I hosted Bruce Springsteen's first radio interview right here. He and Clarence Clemons crammed themselves into this booth, Clarence's sax-case jabbing my knee, and they ran through 'Rosalita' like gladiators on the brink. Bruce leaned into the mic and yelled, 'This is my first radio interview, and my mother's watching from California—Hi, Mom!' In that moment I knew—I felt it in my bones—that something real was happening."  
I lift a water glass—clear and sweating with condensation—because even talk radio veterans need to hydrate before pouring out their souls. My other hand flicks across the turntable's tonearm, cleaning the needle with an anti-static cloth worn as thin as a prayer flag. These machines deserve ritual, not retirement. In their grooves and neon meters lie pulsebeats and confessions no digital screen could ever capture.  
But tonight, I want to tell you about another night—March 15, 1979—when I watched the promise of rock and roll shatter in a high school auditorium. Two boys, worlds apart, destined to collide over a single question: what is authentic rock and roll?  
The console lights wink at me like expectant stars. I've loaded carts of Aerosmith's "Dream On," Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody," The Cars, The J. Geils Band—all the anthems that built us. But first, I need to rewind further, to when the future legends were still untitled demos.  
"Let me introduce you to Nick Johnson and Jackie Marsh," I say, sliding a cart from its slot: "Nick & Jackie—1979 Post Battle of the Bands—4:43—NEVER AIRED." The plastic case is scuffed, my looping cursive scrawled on its label. Inside lies four minutes and forty-three seconds of harmonies spun from high-school bravado and backyard rehearsals—songs that faded before they could ever echo beyond one gymnasium.  
"They should have made it," I whisper, more to myself than to the microphone. "Born on opposite sides of a brook that might as well have been the Atlantic Ocean—Nick, a factory kid with calloused fingers and a sneer; Jackie, the prep-school son of privilege with perfect posture and a longing he couldn't articulate. Neither one had ever met anyone like the other until they shared a stage that night."  
I let the silence stretch, letting the hiss of idle tape underscore the gravity of what follows. Radio engineers once trembled at silence; I've learned it can speak louder than any riff.  
"This is the story of what happens when American class warfare crashes into raw teenage friendship, when disco is gasping its final breaths and punk is unlocking its screeching fury. It's about a town called Sudbury—twenty miles west of here—where two seventeen-year-old boys armed with guitars thought they could redraw the lines of the world. But the world wasn't ready, and by the end of that night, not only their guitars lay broken."  
The ON AIR light pulses with diminishing patience. Four hours remain to excavate the truth of what we lost, what we chose, and what still matters if anyone dares to tune in.  
"But first," I say, laying down a demo cylinder of silence to let anticipation burgeon, "you need to understand how we got there. You need to know who Nick and Jackie were before they became cautionary tales—before they were crushed under the weight of expectations and prejudices, before they became proof that some frequencies are stronger than the static we churn out to drown them."  
The red sign glares. The record spins. One last broadcast is about to begin.